{
  "id": "flash_points___liquids",
  "title": "Flash Points - Liquids",
  "description": "The flash point of a chemical substance is the lowest temperature where enough fluid can evaporate to form a combustible concentration of gas. This tool provides flash points for common liquids and fuels at atmospheric pressure.",
  "type": "calculator",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Flash Points - Liquids\n\nThe [flash point](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flash_point) of a chemical substance is the lowest temperature where enough fluid can evaporate to form a combustible concentration of gas.\n\nThe flash point is an indication of how easy a chemical may burn. Materials with higher flash points are less flammable or hazardous than chemicals with lower flash points.\n\n**Note!** - The Auto-Ignition Temperature is not the same as Flash Point. The Auto-Ignition Temperature indicates the minimum temperature required to ignite a gas or vapor in air without a spark or flame being present."
    },
    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "flashpoint_f",
          "label": "Flash Point",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "°F",
          "defaultValue": 0
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "id": "flashpoint_c",
          "label": "Flash Point",
          "unit": "°C",
          "formula": "5/9 * (inputs.flashpoint_f - 32)",
          "format": "decimal"
        }
      ]
    },
